WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.869
Structured events express telemetry as
named fields rather than prose assembled by

2
00:00:04.949 --> 00:00:06.179
string concatenation.

3
00:00:06.259 --> 00:00:08.475
A stable event name identifies the schema.

4
00:00:08.555 --> 00:00:11.693
Fields such as timestamp, service,
environment, severity,

5
00:00:11.773 --> 00:00:15.588
request or trace identifier, actor,
tenant, resource, action, outcome,

6
00:00:15.668 --> 00:00:19.913
and error category let downstream systems
filter and correlate evidence without

7
00:00:19.993 --> 00:00:21.162
brittle text parsing.

8
00:00:21.242 --> 00:00:23.027
Not every event needs every field;

9
00:00:23.107 --> 00:00:26.430
the schema should reflect the questions
that event must answer.

10
00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:28.048
For an authorization denial,

11
00:00:28.128 --> 00:00:31.574
explicit mapping might record the
server-controlled event name,

12
00:00:31.654 --> 00:00:35.653
authenticated actor and tenant
identifiers, resource type and identifier,

13
00:00:35.733 --> 00:00:37.641
attempted action, and deny outcome.

14
00:00:37.721 --> 00:00:41.966
This is safer to review than spreading an
authentication object or ORM entity into

15
00:00:42.046 --> 00:00:42.662
the logger.

16
00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:46.557
Object spread allows unrelated fields
added later to flow into telemetry

17
00:00:46.637 --> 00:00:47.498
automatically.

18
00:00:47.578 --> 00:00:51.762
Express request and response objects are
especially hazardous because they can

19
00:00:51.842 --> 00:00:55.350
contain headers, cookies, bodies, sockets,
framework internals,

20
00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:56.968
and custom middleware state.

21
00:00:57.048 --> 00:00:59.817
TypeScript can make event construction
consistent.

22
00:00:59.897 --> 00:01:04.204
Discriminated event types can require an
outcome and constrain the fields allowed

23
00:01:04.284 --> 00:01:05.453
for each event family.

24
00:01:05.533 --> 00:01:09.471
A helper can bind approved request context
to a child logger or return a small

25
00:01:09.551 --> 00:01:10.966
security-context object.

26
00:01:11.046 --> 00:01:13.200
These types improve developer feedback,

27
00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.541
but they do not validate runtime input or
remove unsafe values.

28
00:01:16.621 --> 00:01:20.559
A type assertion applied to a request body
changes only the compiler's view;

29
00:01:20.639 --> 00:01:22.362
it does not transform the object.

30
00:01:22.442 --> 00:01:24.411
Values from requests, dependencies,

31
00:01:24.491 --> 00:01:28.921
and deserialized messages still need
runtime validation and deliberate selection.

32
00:01:29.001 --> 00:01:33.309
Use explicit telemetry mapping with the
same discipline used for database writes.

33
00:01:33.389 --> 00:01:37.019
Define a finite vocabulary for event names
and severity on the server.

34
00:01:37.099 --> 00:01:39.806
Keep high-cardinality values out of metric
labels,

35
00:01:39.886 --> 00:01:43.640
even when those values are acceptable in
event logs or trace attributes.

36
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:47.720
Document field meaning, format,
sensitivity, and retention expectations.

37
00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:51.061
Structured logging preserves
machine-readable boundaries,

38
00:01:51.141 --> 00:01:55.202
but its security comes from both the
structure and the decision to include only

39
00:01:55.282 --> 00:01:57.620
fields with justified investigative value.
